<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.3: Quadratic Equations and Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02021</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.3: Quadratic Equations and Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f9c3f891-b8a6-4229-8345-6f17343838bd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students are given a set of equations and graphs of quadratic functions and are asked to match them. To do so, students apply their understanding about the connections between the two representations. To make the matches, they rely on the structure of a quadratic expression and how it relates to the graph, so technology is not an appropriate tool here.</para>
<para>As students work, encourage them to refine their descriptions of the equations and graphs using more precise language and mathematical terms. They also practice explaining their reasoning and critiquing the reasoning of others as they take turns matching and supporting their matches.</para>
<para>As students discuss, listen for strategies they use to match the representations. Highlight productive and reliable strategies as well as use of precise language in the discussion.</para>
<para>Here are the equations and graphs for reference and planning:</para>
<para>\(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\)</para>
<para>\(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\)</para>
<para>\(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\)</para>
<para>\(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\)</para>
<para>\(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\)</para>
<para>\(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\)</para>
<image height="450" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f38d59f200df6a983128aa7f5619f7153bdd8c78" width="650"/><title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of two. Give each group a set of <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/35081cec25e7fdf19783a8242062c143427ebe67">cut-up cards</link>. Ask students to take turns matching a card containing an equation with a graph that represents it. The first partner identifies a match and explains why they think it is a match, while the other listens and works to understand. Then, they switch roles. If they disagree, they should discuss their thinking and work to reach an agreement. Ask students to record each match (write the equation and sketch the graph) and their reasoning. If time is limited, students may just record the label of the graph instead of sketching.</para>
<para>Because this activity is designed to be completed without technology, ask students to put away any devices.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Conversing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Use this routine to help students describe the reasons for their matches. Arrange students in groups of two. Invite Partner A to begin with this sentence frame: “Equation _____ matches with the graph _____ because . . . .” Invite the listener, Partner B, to press for additional details referring to specific features of the graphs and equations, such as vertex, steepness, intercepts, and direction of the opening. Students should switch roles after each match.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making; Cultivate conversation</para>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para><title>Representation: Internalize Comprehension</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Chunk this task into more manageable parts to differentiate the degree of difficulty or complexity by beginning with fewer cards. For example, give students a subset of the cards to start with and introduce the remaining cards once students have completed their initial set of matches.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Conceptual processing; Organization</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Your teacher will give you a set of cards. Each card contains an equation or a graph that represents a quadratic function. Take turns matching each equation to a graph that represents the same function.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> For each pair of cards that you match, explain to your partner how you know they belong together. </item>
  <item> For each pair of cards that your partner matches, listen carefully to their explanation. If you disagree, discuss your thinking and work to reach an agreement. </item>
  <item> Once all the cards are matched, record the equation, the letter of the equation, and a sketch of the corresponding graph, and write a brief note or explanation about how you knew they were a match. </item>
</list>
<image height="307" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9e3666853195ea17931a06f0d3d0c0ea93498bf8" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\)</para>
<image height="307" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d466c76748bb2ecb4bcf5c219c37a8d42e389ad" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\)</para>
<image height="309" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d28232a07fc49824d62edd58f17311938ace6810" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\)</para>
<image height="419" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6aba729ee1a38d4bff05e4f6d2a781e6f85c6d9f" width="419"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\)</para>
<image height="298" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e26776c65d185b7d6ef47ffebf88d403f2ecf7e2" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\)</para>
<image height="298" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bccc7cd3f38774bf699d865ccb8eed107cb66e59" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the equation that matches this graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(f(x)=(x−1)^2+4\) </item>
  <item> \(g(x)=-(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(h(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(p(x)=-(x+1)^2−4\) </item>
  <item> \(q(x)=2(x−4)^2+1\) </item>
  <item> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(r(x)=(x+4)^2−1\)</para>
<title>Video: Learning About Quadratic Equations and Graphs</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about quadratic equations and graphs.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ed46d4e172a9596e0ebddd22dd41d4482020ea61">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/699de55621cdcd90927da9dbf409c144e43650c8" srclang="en_us">https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ed46d4e172a9596e0ebddd22dd41d4482020ea61
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Some students may be challenged to identify the coordinates of the vertex on the graphs because the \(x\)- and \(y\)-axes use a different scale. Encourage students to write down coordinates of the vertex and the intercepts to help them match the graphs to the equations. Some students may have trouble identifying that one graph is steeper than the others. Provide them with graph paper and a piece of patty-paper or a transparency and have them trace one graph onto it. They can overlay the traced graph on top of the other graphs to figure out which one is steeper. Students used similar tools to explore transformations in middle school.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share aspects of the equations and graphs they found helpful for the matching. If not mentioned in students' explanations, highlight the following features:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The number being added or subtracted from \(x\) in the squared term, or the \(h\) in \((x−h)^2\):
    <list class="os-raise-noindent">
      <item> If \(h\) is being subtracted from \(x\), \(h\) is a positive number and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is positive. </item>
      <item> If \(h\) is being added to \(x\) in the expression, then \(h\) is a negative number and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is negative. </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item> The constant term in the quadratic expression in vertex form, or the \(k\) in \(a(x−h)^2+k\):
    <list class="os-raise-noindent">
      <item> If \(k\) is positive, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is also positive. </item>
      <item> If \(k\) is negative, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is also negative. </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item> The coefficient of the squared term, or the \(a\) in \(a(x−h)^2+k\): We saw earlier that when the squared term has a negative coefficient, it makes all positive values negative. This means that:
    <list class="os-raise-noindent">
      <item> If \(a\) is negative, the vertex represents the maximum (the graph of the quadratic function opens downward). </item>
      <item> If \(a\) is positive, the vertex represents the minimum (the graph of the quadratic function opens upward). </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item> The magnitude of \(a\):
    <list class="os-raise-noindent">
      <item> The larger \(a\) is, the narrower the opening of the graph is (or the "steeper" the graph is). </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>The range of the function:
    <list>
      <item>If the vertex is a maximum, then the range is less than or equal to the \(y\)-value of the vertex. So, \(y \leq k\)</item>
      <item>If the vertex is a minimum, then the range is greater than or equal to the \(y\)-value of the vertex. So, \(y \geq k\).</item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para>Students may also reason the other way around: by looking at the graphs first and then relate the features of the graphs to the equations.</para>
<title>7.16.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following equations matches the graph below?</para>
<image height="306" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d77bd4775e34e02716bc6ffdbfc8e5dfd9ded166" width="300"/><table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Answers
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Feedback
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(y=(x-3)^2+2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The parabola opens down. The answer is \(y=-(x-3)^2+2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(y=-(x-3)^2+2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: The parabola opens down, so the value of \(a\) should be negative. The vertex is at \((3,2)\) so this is the correct vertex form for the graph.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(y=-(x+3)^2+2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This quadratic has a vertex at \((-3, 2)\) but the graph has a vertex at \((3, 2)\). The answer is \(y=-(x-3)^2+2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(y=(x+3)^2+2\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This quadratic has a vertex at \((-3, 2)\) but the graph has a vertex at \((3, 2)\) and opens down. The answer is \(y=-(x-3)^2+2\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>7.16.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Matching Graphs and Equations</title>
<para>When matching graphs of quadratic functions and their equations, look for the matching vertex. Then look if the parabola is opening up or down. Finally, use another point to determine if the graph has been compressed or stretched. Recall this type of transformation will affect the \(a\)-value of the equation.</para>
<para>Looking at the graph below, choose the equation that matches the graph.</para>
<image height="296" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e8f20cb5a53f3f5adc16739395465feb2a796acf" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(y=(x-2)^2-3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x+2)^2-3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-(x+2)^2-3\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-(x-2)^2-3\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the vertex.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Does the parabola open up or down?</para>
<para>The correct answer is b. \(y=(x+2)^2-3\).</para>
<para>Remember the vertex form is \(y = a(x - h)^2 + k\). If the \(x\)-variable is being added in the parenthesis, the parabola has been shifted left.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the vertex form of the equation and write the parts you know: the coordinates of the vertex.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - To solve for \(a\), find another point on the graph of the parabola and plug in those values for \(x\) and \(y\) in the equation you wrote.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Plug the value for \(a\) back into the equation.</para>
<title>Try It: Matching Graphs and Equations</title>
<para>Match the graph with its equation in vertex form.</para>
<image height="296" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c73dfd9bb3e24c529a0745e0a8a4eacd6bd86603" width="300"/><list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(y=(x+1)^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-(x+1)^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-(x-1)^2+2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x-1)^2+2\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to match a graph and its equation:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the vertex.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Does the parabola open up or down?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the vertex form of the equation and write the parts you know: the coordinates of the vertex.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - To solve for \(a\), find another point on the graph of the parabola and plug in those values for \(x\) and \(y\) in the equation you wrote.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\).</para>
<para>\(3=a(-1)^2+2\)</para>
<para>\(3=a+2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Plug the value for \(a\) back into the equation.</para></section>
